id     = "roll-rate"
about  = """A finding of round 4 (docs/decisions.md roll-rate): motion follows time,
not the frame rate (design.md "Testing": the injectable 60 Hz and 144 Hz frame
clock). A `roll: true` number on the default spring rolled for 233 ms at 60 Hz
and 174 ms at 144 Hz, while a 300 ms tween rolled 300 ms at both: a spring's
end was read off one frame's position and velocity, and the underdamped
`$motion.spatial` passes near its target slowly at each turning point, a window
that 144 Hz frames landed in and 60 Hz ones missed. A spring now comes to rest
when its amplitude (position and velocity together) is within its tolerance,
a moment of its curve alone. The same roll, and a tween beside it, run at both
rates, and each rest is held to one wall-clock bound at both (180 ms for the
spring, 320 ms for the tween): 11 ticks at 60 Hz and 26 at 144 Hz, 20 and
47 (`settle`; eval/mutants/roll-rate.patch reverts the fix)."""
screen = { width = 200, scale = 1.0 }
hz     = 60

=== shell.strand
state n = "41"
state m = "41"
bar Top {
  height: 30
  row { height: 30; gap: 12; align: center; pad: 0, 8
    text n { id: sprung; color: #ffffff; roll: true }
    text m { id: timed; color: #ffffff; roll: true ~ 300ms }
  }
}

=== steps
rest
mark spring-60
set n "42"
rest max 11
mark tween-60
set m "42"
rest max 20
hz 144
rest
mark spring-144
set n "43"
rest max 26
mark tween-144
set m "43"
rest max 47
